{"records":[{"id":"bwphph29kv","title":"The stoichiometry of the eIF4F complex is important for the activation of the integrated stress response","description":"Eukaryotes have evolved independent conserved pathways to regulate translation initiation in response to stress. One is the activation of the integrated stress response (ISR) via phosphorylation of the alpha subunit of the initiation factor eIF2 by kinases that sense biotic and abiotic stresses. This leads to reduction of initiator ternary complex and hence inhibition of global translation initiation. Paradoxically however, eIF2 phosphorylation promotes the translation of pro-survival effector genes, such as GCN4 in yeast. Complementing the ISR is the Target of Rapamycin (TOR) signaling pathway, whose inhibition leads to sequestration of the initiation factor eIF4E and subsequent inhibition of canonical initiation. Here we probe translational control in the absence of eIF4E by utilizing a temperature-sensitive allele of the factor in Saccharomyces cerevisiae. Intriguingly, we find that loss of eIF4E leads to translation of GCN4. In addition, we find that translation of GCN4 is neither accompanied by eIF2α phosphorylation nor reduction in initiator ternary complex. Our data suggest that when eIF4E levels are depleted, GCN4 translation is de-repressed via a unique mechanism that involves faster scanning by the small ribosome subunit due to increased local concentration of eIF4A . Overall, our findings suggest that the stoichiometry of eIF4F components is key to ribosome dynamics and may play important roles in translational control of gene expression. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-12-06T06:35:07.231Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/bwphph29kv","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/bwphph29kv"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Kyusik","last_name":"Kim","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ankanahalli N","last_name":"Nanjaraj Urs","orcid":"0000-0002-1357-6733"},{"first_name":"Victor","last_name":"Lasehinde","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alison ","last_name":"Greenlaw","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Benjamin ","last_name":"Hudson","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Hani","last_name":"Zaher","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Protein Synthesis","Ribosome","Cellular Stress Response","Initiation Complex","Yeast Model"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"fch5m3kt42","title":"Endosomal trafficking and DNA damage checkpoint kinases dictate survival to replication stress by regulating amino acid uptake and protein synthesis","description":"Atg6/Beclin 1 mediates autophagy and endosomal trafficking. We investigated how Atg6 influences replication stress. Combining genetic, genomic, metabolomic and proteomic approaches we found that the Vps34-Vps15-Atg6/Beclin 1-Vps38/UVRAG-phosphatydilinositol-3 phosphate (PtdIns(3)P) axis sensitizes cells to replication stress by favoring the degradation of plasma membrane amino acid (AA) transporters via endosomal trafficking and ESCRT proteins, while the PtdIns(3)P phosphatases Ymr1 and Inp53 promote survival to replication stress by reversing this process. An impaired AA uptake triggers activation of Gcn2, which attenuates protein synthesis by phosphorylating eIF2α. Mec1/Atr-Rad53/Chk1/Chk2 activation during replication stress further hinders translation efficiency by counteracting eIF2α dephosphorylation through Glc7/PP1. AA shortage-induced hyperphosphorylation of eIF2α inhibits the synthesis of 65 stress response proteins, thus resulting in cell sensitization to replication stress, while TORC1 promotes cell survival. \nOur findings reveal an integrated network mediated by endosomal trafficking, translational control pathways and checkpoint kinases linking AA availability to the response to replication stress. \n\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-09-14T19:17:15.019Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/fch5m3kt42","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/fch5m3kt42"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Arta","last_name":"Ajazi","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Marco","last_name":"Foiani","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Genetics","DNA Replication","Protein Synthesis","Membrane Protein Trafficking","Amino Acids","Yeast","Metabolism"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9dhbprzxh2","title":"Part1_Petromurin C induces protective autophagy and apoptosis in FLT3-ITD-positive AML: synergy with gilteritinib","description":"Treatment of acute myeloid leukemia (AML) remains inefficient due to drug resistance and relapse, particularly in patients with FMS-like tyrosine kinase (FLT)3-internal tandem duplication (ITD). Marine-derived natural products have recently been used for drug development against AML. We show here that petromurin C, isolated from the culture extract of the marine-derived fungus Aspergillus candidus KUFA0062, which was isolated from the marine sponge Epipolasis sp., induces early autophagy followed by apoptotic cell death via activation of the intrinsic cell death pathway concomitant with mitochondrial stress and downregulation of Mcl-1 in FLT3-ITD mutated MV4-11 cells. Moreover, petromurin C synergized with the clinically-used FLT3 inhibitor gilteritinib at sub-toxic concentrations. Altogether our results suggest that petromurin C could act as an anti-leukemic agent alone or in combination with gilteritinib.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-12-13T11:09:36.344Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9dhbprzxh2","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9dhbprzxh2"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"You Na ","last_name":"Ha","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Health Sciences"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"z4m2d88hgz","title":"Part2_Petromurin C induces protective autophagy and apoptosis in FLT3-ITD-positive AML: synergy with gilteritinib","description":"Treatment of acute myeloid leukemia (AML) remains inefficient due to drug resistance and relapse, particularly in patients with FMS-like tyrosine kinase 3 (FLT3)-internal tandem duplication (ITD). Marine-derived natural products have recently been used for drug development against AML. We show here that petromurin C, isolated from the culture extract of the marine-derived fungus Aspergillus candidus KUFA0062, which was isolated from the marine sponge Epipolasis sp., induces early autophagy followed by apoptotic cell death via activation of the intrinsic cell death pathway concomitant with mitochondrial stress and downregulation of Mcl-1 in FLT3-ITD mutated MV4-11 cells. Moreover, petromurin C synergized with the clinically-used FLT3 inhibitor gilteritinib at sub-toxic concentrations. Altogether our results suggest that petromurin C could act as an anti-leukemic agent alone or in combination with gilteritinib.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-12-13T11:12:18.026Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/z4m2d88hgz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/z4m2d88hgz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"You Na ","last_name":"Ha","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Health Sciences"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"xzjmk67vnc","title":"Part3_Petromurin C induces protective autophagy and apoptosis in FLT3-ITD-positive AML: synergy with gilteritinib","description":"Treatment of acute myeloid leukemia (AML) remains inefficient due to drug resistance and relapse, particularly in patients with FMS-like tyrosine kinase 3 (FLT3)-internal tandem duplication (ITD). Marine-derived natural products have recently been used for drug development against AML. We show here that petromurin C, isolated from the culture extract of the marine-derived fungus Aspergillus candidus KUFA0062, which was isolated from the marine sponge Epipolasis sp., induces early autophagy followed by apoptotic cell death via activation of the intrinsic cell death pathway concomitant with mitochondrial stress and downregulation of Mcl-1 in FLT3-ITD mutated MV4-11 cells. Moreover, petromurin C synergized with the clinically-used FLT3 inhibitor gilteritinib at sub-toxic concentrations. Altogether our results suggest that petromurin C could act as an anti-leukemic agent alone or in combination with gilteritinib.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-12-13T11:12:45.533Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/xzjmk67vnc","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/xzjmk67vnc"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"You Na ","last_name":"Ha","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Health Sciences"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"5thj4jyjkd","title":"AMP accumulation during mitochondrial stress induces transcription of cytosolic and mitochondrial protein synthesis components via NHR-180","description":"The mitochondrial unfolded protein response (UPRmt), which promotes mitochondrial biogenesis and proteostasis, is regulated by the transcription factor ATFS-1. In C. elegans, impaired atfs-1 expression perturbs mitochondrial function and slows development. Here, via an RNAi suppressor screen, we found that inhibition of the nuclear hormone receptor NHR-180 increases mitochondrial function and the rate of development in atfs-1(null) worms. NHR-180 is activated during mitochondrial dysfunction and induces transcription of genes required for protein synthesis on cytosolic and mitochondrial ribosomes. Importantly, the NHR-180 ligand binding domain interacts with AMP, which results in increased protein synthesis, potentially to promote mitochondrial biogenesis and recovery from transient mitochondrial perturbations.\nHowever, NHR-180 activation exacerbates the mitochondrial dysfunction caused by mutations in genes required for oxidative phosphorylation. Consistent with these findings, we found that inhibition of the S6 kinase homolog RSKS-1 also increased mitochondrial function in atfs-1(null) worms. Importantly, treatment with an S6 kinase inhibitor also increased respiration and mtDNA content in mammalian ATF5-knockout cells and OXPHOS-deficient models, suggesting that translation regulation is a conserved, targetable approach for restoring mitochondrial function in disease.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-08-28T09:50:52.919Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/5thj4jyjkd","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/5thj4jyjkd"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Avijit","last_name":"Mallick","orcid":"0000-0002-9423-7134"},{"first_name":"Cole ","last_name":"Haynes","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"k3fsfzr7j8","title":"Prkra dimer senses double-stranded RNAs to dictate global translation efficiency","description":"Double-stranded RNAs (dsRNAs), known as conserved pathogen-associated molecular patterns, activate the integrated stress response via interferon-induced protein kinase R (PKR), leading to global translation inhibition. However, the interferon system is inactive in pluripotent cells, leaving the mechanisms of dsRNA sensing and translational control unclear. In this study, we utilized early zebrafish embryos as a model of pluripotent cells and discovered a PKR-independent blockage of translation initiation by dsRNA stimulation. Prkra dimer was identified as the genuine dsRNA sensor. Upon dsRNA binding, the dimerized dsRNA binding domain 3 of Prkra becomes activated to sequester the eIF2 complexes from the translation machinery, inhibiting global protein synthesis. This distinctive embryonic stress response restricts RNA virus replication in zebrafish embryos, is conserved in mouse embryonic stem cells, and compensates PKR function in differentiated cells. Therefore, the Prkra-mediated dsRNA sensing and translation control may serve as a common strategy for cells to adapt environmental stresses.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-03-28T13:23:10.265Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/k3fsfzr7j8","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/k3fsfzr7j8"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"tong","last_name":"lu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Stem Cell","Embryonic Stem Cell","Gene Translation","Sensor","Double-Stranded RNA"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"f84wfs2j72","title":"GCLM lactylation mediated by ACAT2 promotes ferroptosis resistance in KRASG12D-mutant cancer","description":"This data consists of raw immunoblotting images from the article titled \"KGCLM lactylation mediated by ACAT2 promotes ferroptosis resistance in KRASG12D-mutant cancer\". KRAS mutations drive tumorigenesis, but their role in ferroptosis regulation remains unclear. Here, we construct KRASWT and KRASG12D-mutant cancer cells and demonstrate that G12D-mutant cells exhibit increased viability and reduced ferroptosis upon RSL3 or erastin treatment. These cells show diminished lipid peroxidation and mitochondrial damage, indicating ferroptosis resistance. KRASG12D activates MEK-ERK signaling to phosphorylate LDHA, enhancing glycolysis and lactate production. Exogenous lactate supplementation similarly protects wild-type cells from ferroptosis. Mechanistically, G12D mutation-derived lactate induces Glutamate-cysteine ligase modifier (GCLM) lactylation, a process catalysed by Acetyl-CoA Acetyltransferase 2 (ACAT2). Inhibition of GCLM lactylation either through the mutation of the lactylation site or by knockdown of ACAT2 diminished the enzymatic activity of Glutamate-cysteine ligase (GCL) and suppressed Glutathione synthesis. Importantly, ACAT2 depletion overcomes ferroptosis resistance in KRASG12D-mutant tumors in vivo. Our findings reveal a KRASG12D-driven metabolic adaptation linking GCLM lactylation to ferroptosis resistance, proposing ACAT2 inhibition as a therapeutic strategy for KRAS-mutant cancers. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-05-09T14:20:53.296Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/f84wfs2j72","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/f84wfs2j72"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"裕斌","last_name":"陈","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Pancreatic Cancer"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"58chtf922v","title":"GCLM lactylation mediated by ACAT2 promotes ferroptosis resistance in KRASG12D-mutant cancer","description":"This data consists of raw immunoblotting images from the article titled \"KGCLM lactylation mediated by ACAT2 promotes ferroptosis resistance in KRASG12D-mutant cancer\". KRAS mutations drive tumorigenesis, but their role in ferroptosis regulation remains unclear. Here, we construct KRASWT and KRASG12D-mutant cancer cells and demonstrate that G12D-mutant cells exhibit increased viability and reduced ferroptosis upon RSL3 or erastin treatment. These cells show diminished lipid peroxidation and mitochondrial damage, indicating ferroptosis resistance. KRASG12D activates MEK-ERK signaling to phosphorylate LDHA, enhancing glycolysis and lactate production. Exogenous lactate supplementation similarly protects wild-type cells from ferroptosis. Mechanistically, G12D mutation-derived lactate induces Glutamate-cysteine ligase modifier (GCLM) lactylation, a process catalysed by Acetyl-CoA Acetyltransferase 2 (ACAT2). Inhibition of GCLM lactylation either through the mutation of the lactylation site or by knockdown of ACAT2 diminished the enzymatic activity of Glutamate-cysteine ligase (GCL) and suppressed Glutathione synthesis. Importantly, ACAT2 depletion overcomes ferroptosis resistance in KRASG12D-mutant tumors in vivo. Our findings reveal a KRASG12D-driven metabolic adaptation linking GCLM lactylation to ferroptosis resistance, proposing ACAT2 inhibition as a therapeutic strategy for KRAS-mutant cancers. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-05-06T05:22:29.855Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/58chtf922v","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/58chtf922v"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"裕斌","last_name":"陈","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Pancreatic Cancer"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"fx9zk86wf6","title":"Deubiquitylase OTUD3 regulates integrated stress response to suppress progression and sorafenib resistance of liver cancer. Lingqiang Zhang, et al.","description":"Original Data of our manuscript entitled “Deubiquitylase OTUD3 regulates integrated stress response to suppress progression and sorafenib resistance of liver cancer”.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-07-10T09:31:06.58Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/fx9zk86wf6","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/fx9zk86wf6"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Hongmiao","last_name":"Dai","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Liver Cancer","Ubiquitylation"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":140028,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":170,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":140028,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":139286,"restricted":68,"embargoed":674},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2346},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1422},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":692},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":482},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":444},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":427},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":413},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":393},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":334},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":277}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":137372},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1911},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":188},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":138},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":78},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":66},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":58},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":41},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":31},{"id":"5f1edf21-7948-4d82-894f-c0ed4b1e8f52","name":"Teesside University","count":25}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"9d63ad1d-c391-5c35-a12e-cafe17017098","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid","count":955},{"id":"ecb64011-71f0-5223-b4e4-4d27d5f51d83","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid Centro de Biologia Molecular Severo Ochoa","count":917},{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":532},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":408},{"id":"55ba66b2-f276-5204-a3d8-acfa499794e0","name":"The University of Manchester","count"